{"name":"Julep","hex":"#57aa80","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"julep","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A refreshing drink flavored with aromatic herbs, especially mint, and sometimes alcohol.","A pleasant-tasting liquid medicine in which other nauseous medicines are taken."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/julep"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mint julep","blocks":[{"text":"Mint julep is an alcoholic cocktail, consisting primarily of bourbon, sugar, water, crushed or shaved ice, and fresh mint. As a bourbon-based cocktail, it is associated with the American South and the cuisine of the Southern United States in general, in particular as a symbol of the Kentucky Derby."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Mint_Julep_im_Silberbecher.jpg/330px-Mint_Julep_im_Silberbecher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mint_julep","wikidata":"Q616477"},{"title":"Mint Julep (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Mint Julep is a 2010 romance film written, directed, and produced by Ian Teal and Kathy Fehl, starring David Morse, Angelica Torn, and James Gandolfini. It is set in small-town North Carolina and follows the struggling marriage of Deirdre, a diner waitress played by Torn, and Leighton, an insurance salesman played by Teal. Morse plays an odd groundskeeper at a golf course who witnesses a murder, and Gandolfini plays a landlord in New York City."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mint_Julep_(film)","wikidata":"Q6869557"},{"title":"Julep (company)","blocks":[{"text":"Julep Beauty, Inc. is a New York based cosmetics company founded in 2007 by Jane Park. The company sells its products online and in its own branded beauty parlors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Julep_logo.svg/330px-Julep_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julep_(company)","wikidata":"Q21750314"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Mint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg/500px-Mint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Mint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg/1280px-Mint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Mint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg/500px-Mint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A mint julep made with Henry Clay's original recipe at the Round Robin Bar. According to bartender and historian Jim Hewes, the cocktail was originally served in a crystal glass because it represented a more upper-class beverage.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMint_Julep_at_the_Round_Robin_Bar_-_Stierch.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Silver_mint_julep_cup.jpg/500px-Silver_mint_julep_cup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Silver_mint_julep_cup.jpg/1280px-Silver_mint_julep_cup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Silver_mint_julep_cup.jpg/500px-Silver_mint_julep_cup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A silver mint julep cup","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver_mint_julep_cup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Mint_Julep_-_Round_Robin_Bar_-_2018_-_Stierch_02.jpg/500px-Mint_Julep_-_Round_Robin_Bar_-_2018_-_Stierch_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or